course: dictionary
lesson: 196
title: The word aisle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An aisle is a long, narrow passage with rows of seats or shelves on either side of it. You can find aisles in a church, a theatre, a plane, a bus, a classroom, a supermarket and a library. When you meet the word, look at the place around it to work out which kind of aisle it means. «Sam ran up the aisle and knocked over the candles» tells you he was in a church, because candles and a vicar are there.
explain: Aisle is a tricky word because it sounds like I'll, but it is spelled differently. Some words that sound alike have different spellings and meanings, so check the spelling when you read or write. You can also say «aisle seat» for a seat next to the passage, and «window seat» for a seat next to the window.
explain: Some phrases use aisle in a special way. «Walk down the aisle» means to get married in a church, because the bride walks along the church aisle. «Rolling in the aisles» means laughing very hard. «Aisle 5» in a shop tells you where to find the cleaning products. Context helps: read the whole sentence to find out which meaning is correct.
fact: An aisle is a long, narrow passage between rows of seats or shelves.
fact: You can find aisles in churches, theatres, planes, buses, classrooms, supermarkets and libraries.
fact: «Walk down the aisle» means to get married in a church.
fact: «Rolling in the aisles» means laughing very hard.
fact: Keep aisles clear of bags, so nobody trips and people can leave quickly in an emergency.
exercise: «Sam ran up the aisle and knocked over the candles.» Where was Sam? | *In a church | In a swimming pool | On a farm | In a bakery
exercise: «She wanted to walk down the aisle in a long white dress.» What does walk down the aisle mean here? | Leave the church quickly | Go shopping for a dress | *Get married in a church | Walk slowly down the stairs
exercise: On a plane, what is an aisle seat? | Under the seat in front | In the cockpit with the pilot | Next to the window | *Next to the passage
test: Why must you keep the aisles clear of bags? | *So people do not trip and can leave quickly | So the bags can be seen from far away | So the floor stays dry | So the shop can sell more bags
test: «The audience was rolling in the aisles.» What does rolling in the aisles mean? | Falling over and hurt | Rolling a ball down the walkway | *Laughing very hard | Clapping slowly
exam: «His mum laughed so loudly that a man in the next aisle turned round.» What does aisle mean in this sentence? | A bus ticket | A loud noise | *A long passage between rows of seats or shelves | A type of bread
